WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:02.405
This example shows a very common design

00:00:02.708 --> 00:00:05.089
a generated surrogate key plus a protected

00:00:05.116 --> 00:00:10.773
natural identifier PostgreSQL generates patient ID

00:00:10.786 --> 00:00:14.355
automatically That gives us a stable internal

00:00:14.368 --> 00:00:16.460
key that can be used in relationships

00:00:18.211 --> 00:00:20.474
The clinic's medical record number still has

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.763
business meaning so it receives a UNIQUE

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:26.029
constraint The surrogate key does not make

00:00:26.055 --> 00:00:30.625
the natural business identifier irrelevant Run the

00:00:30.651 --> 00:00:33.733
example in the browser Notice that the

00:00:33.746 --> 00:00:35.891
INSERT does not provide a patient ID

00:00:35.904 --> 00:00:40.625
because PostgreSQL generates it Then inspect the

00:00:40.638 --> 00:00:44.666
returned row and the table structure If

00:00:44.692 --> 00:00:47.495
you copy this example into pgAdmin focus

00:00:47.521 --> 00:00:49.679
on the two different roles patient ID

00:00:49.692 --> 00:00:52.100
is the internal row identity while MRN

00:00:52.113 --> 00:00:53.784
is protected business identity
